         location.pathname itself rather than this file being templated.
---
<div class="max-w-[960px] mx-auto">
  <!-- Picker/menu row before the brand row, and align-left on
       <climbing-header> -- matches /logbook's own layout (left-aligned
       brand row below the header row, not centered above it). Found via
       Raven's production report, 2026-08-10 -- see climbing-header.js's
       own comment on the align-left attribute. -->
  {% include "page-header.njk" %}

  <div class="flex items-end gap-3 mb-2" id="picker-tabs-row">
    <climbing-discipline-picker></climbing-discipline-picker>
    <climbing-tab-bar active-page="performance"></climbing-tab-bar>
  </div>

  <!-- #913 -- back-link utility (styles/tailwind.css), same pattern
       across every report page and My Account's own back links; href set
       client-side in client/performance-rpe-main.js once USERNAME is known. -->
  <a class="back-link mt-4 mb-5" id="back-to-performance-link" href="#">
    <svg class="w-[.9rem] h-[.9rem] stroke-current fill-none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m12 19-7-7 7-7"></path><path d="M19 12H5"></path></svg>
    Performance Insights
  </a>

  <h1 class="page-title">Effort / RPE Trend</h1>

  <!-- #620 -- report-style prose, not a bordered/background card --
       same <climbing-grade-pyramid>'s own #window-note pattern (flowing
       paragraphs, inline <strong> emphasis, no box). The mirror image of
       #597's own per-field entry-form captions: this names which field
       feeds this view. -->
  <div class="mb-5" id="view-explainer">
    <p class="text-[.95rem] text-foreground leading-[1.7]">How hard your sends have felt (self-rated exertion) alongside how hard they were (max grade sent), by time period — so you can see whether extra effort is buying real progress.</p>
    <p class="text-[.82rem] text-muted leading-[1.7] mt-2">Bars are your average <strong class="text-foreground font-semibold">Exertion %</strong> per period; the overlaid line marks the toughest grade you sent that period, labeled directly on its points. Built from the <strong class="text-foreground font-semibold">Exertion</strong> slider on each send -- RPE readings also tend to read less reliable for newer or lower-grade climbers (Gajdošík et al., 2020), so take this trend as a loose signal, not a precise measure.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- #111 -- Performance insights are online-only, deliberately, not a
       client-side fallback computed from possibly-stale cached entries
       (Raven's own call: at the crag with poor/no connection, the
       priority is reading/updating the logbook, not reviewing aggregate
       stats -- a "review later" activity). Shown instead of the
       RPE/effort trend view when the initial fetch fails; starts
       hidden, toggled in performance-rpe-main.js's own boot(). -->
  <div class="row-card text-center max-w-[420px] mx-auto" id="performance-offline" hidden>
    <p class="text-[.9rem] text-foreground">Performance insights need a connection.</p>
    <p class="text-[.82rem] text-muted mt-2">Reconnect and reload this page to see your effort trend.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
    <div id="time-window-root"></div>
    <!-- #704 -- which of this discipline's scales the chart below
         renders grades in; client/report-grade-scale-picker.js. -->
    <div id="report-grade-scale-root"></div>
  </div>
  <div id="rpe-root"></div>

  <!-- #797 -- replaces the old evidence-tier chip + popup (client/
       evidence-tier.js): an inline, always-visible section reads more
       like a real citation than a tap-to-reveal chip, and gives room to
       state the finding's own real caveat (reduced reliability below
       advanced ability levels) directly, not just gesture at "peer-
       reviewed" as a category. -->
  <h2 class="sources-heading">Sources</h2>
  <p class="text-[.82rem] text-muted leading-[1.7] mb-3">This view's own "RPE reads less reliably at lower ability levels" caveat, and its underlying framing of Exertion as a physiological-demand proxy, come from a controlled climbing-specific study:</p>
  <ol class="m-0 pl-[1.2rem] text-[.84rem] leading-[1.6] text-foreground [&>li+li]:mt-[10px]">
    <li>Gajdošík, J., Baláš, J., &amp; Draper, N. (2020). "Effect of Height on Perceived Exertion and Physiological Responses for Climbers of Differing Ability Levels." <em class="text-muted italic">Frontiers in Psychology</em>, 11:997. <a class="text-accent" href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00997/full" target="_blank" rel="noopener">frontiersin.org ↗</a></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="text-center text-muted text-[.78rem] mt-8" id="footer"></div>
</div>
